Number = {11, SI},
Pages = {1362-1376},
Month = {NOV},
Abstract = {Aiming to inform both marine management and the public, coupled
   environmental-climate scenario simulations for the future Baltic Sea are
   analyzed. The projections are performed under two greenhouse gas
   concentration scenarios (medium and high-end) and three nutrient load
   scenarios spanning the range of plausible socio-economic pathways.
   Assuming an optimistic scenario with perfect implementation of the
   Baltic Sea Action Plan (BSAP), the projections suggest that the
   achievement of Good Environmental Status will take at least a few more
   decades. However, for the perception of the attractiveness of beach
   recreational sites, extreme events such as tropical nights,
   record-breaking sea surface temperature (SST), and cyanobacteria blooms
   may be more important than mean ecosystem indicators. Our projections
   suggest that the incidence of record-breaking summer SSTs will increase
   significantly. Under the BSAP, record-breaking cyanobacteria blooms will
   no longer occur in the future, but may reappear at the end of the
   century in a business-as-usual nutrient load scenario.},
